{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790966809,"nonce":"QxXrmmOEXCym6nbZaPaJlz19IRc=","title":"Wheel-bearing rAFs unconditionally invalidating render cache + draining all layer textures","message":"`crates/blinc_app/src/web.rs` Phase 0b ran\n`invalidate_render_cache_tagged(\"had_scroll\")` +\n`drain_all_layer_textures(\"had_scroll\")` on EVERY frame whose\n`pending_wheel_delta` was non-zero. macOS trackpad gestures (and\nSafari's ~800ms momentum tail) produce ~60 wheel-bearing rAFs in a\nsustained zoom; the drain releases every composite-promoted /\nmotion-subtree GPU texture in the tree, and the next paint has to\nre-bake all of them. Cost scales with overlay count, so the visible\nsymptom shows up when content overlaps the HUD or near canvas\nedges — exactly where motion containers / stack-layered overlays\nhave been baked recently.\n\n**Fix shipped 2026-06-19**: track `last_scroll_invalidate_ms` on\n`WebApp`; gate both calls behind a `now - last \u003e 100ms` predicate so\nthey fire ONLY at burst edges (first wheel after a gap), not every\nframe mid-burst. Texture released at burst-start covers the stale-\ncontent-space-coord case the original comment cared about; mid-\nburst frames keep the baked textures and re-blit at the new\nviewport without a release/rebake cycle.\n\n**Why 100ms**: trackpad momentum events arrive ~16ms apart; mouse\nwheel notches arrive ~30-50ms apart on a sustained roll; intentional\n\"new gesture\" gaps are \u003e200ms in user testing. 100ms is comfortably\nabove the within-gesture cadence and well below the between-gesture\ngap.\n\n**Why this matters / when to apply elsewhere**:\n\n- The desktop runner has a similar `scroll_animating`-gated fast\n  path at `windowed.rs:6916-6933` — the same burst-edge principle.\n  Don't recreate per-frame texture drains in any wheel / scroll /\n  zoom path without gating them.\n- The companion synthesis from the zoom-jank workflow listed this\n  as secondary cause #3. The MOST likely cause is the absence of a\n  zoom spring (ZoomController writes viewport.zoom synchronously\n  on each event with no smoothing — see workflow output for the\n  full spring design). The burst-edge gate addresses the immediate\n  HUD-overlap symptom but doesn't fix the underlying per-event\n  step variance; that's the follow-up.\n\n**Related**:\n\n- gotcha dynamic batch aux data — every per-frame wipe forces\n  aux_data re-upload, which has its own gotchas.\n- gotcha overlay canvas z interleave — composite-promoted\n  textures are the ones being drained; their bake order is what\n  produces visible HUD/overlay glitches when re-bake races the\n  blit.\n- The workflow output at\n  `/private/tmp/claude-501/.../tasks/wogblapa2.output` carries the\n  full design for the zoom spring follow-up.","files":null}]}